This explains the root cause.

The key error is:

FailedScheduling
0/1 nodes are available: 1 node(s) were unschedulable.

The scheduler is not rejecting the pod because of CPU, memory, or taints. It is refusing because the only node in the SNO cluster is marked unschedulable (cordoned).

Earlier you showed:

NAME                STATUS
sno1.lab1.ocp.lan   Ready,SchedulingDisabled

SchedulingDisabled means the node is cordoned.

Because this is an SNO cluster:

There is only one node.

The machine-config-operator and machine-config-controller Deployments must run on that node.

Since the node is cordoned, they remain Pending.

Because they are Pending, the MCP never reconciles its status (ObservedGeneration stays at 16).


Verify whether the node is cordoned

Run:

oc get node

or

oc describe node sno1.lab1.ocp.lan | grep Unschedulable

You should see:

Unschedulable: true

Fix it

If you did not intentionally cordon the node, uncordon it:

oc adm uncordon sno1.lab1.ocp.lan

Then verify:

oc get nodes

Expected:

Ready

instead of

Ready,SchedulingDisabled

After uncordoning

Watch these commands:

oc get pods -n openshift-machine-config-operator -w

The following pods should transition from Pending to Running:

machine-config-operator

machine-config-controller


Then monitor:

oc get mcp -w

You should eventually see:

UPDATED   True
UPDATING  False
DEGRADED  False
READYMACHINECOUNT 1

One more thing

Your MCP still has the annotation:

sriovnetwork.openshift.io/state: Paused

Once the node is uncordoned and the MCO pods are running, check whether the SR-IOV Operator clears that annotation automatically. If it does not, we can investigate why the SR-IOV Operator left the MCP in that state.

Please run:

oc adm uncordon sno1.lab1.ocp.lan
oc get nodes
oc get pods -n openshift-machine-config-operator
oc get mcp

Those outputs will confirm whether the cluster recovers after making the node schedulable again.
